s Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc05a0f-f981-5af5-a7e1-d40a3d4c4527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (1,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505155} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1b2c7f-28a2-5287-9aca-dc1c26ca1232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505262} {:answer/id #uuid "97b4b334-2f21-576b-a08b-fd2f13db8840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [1,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505400} {:answer/id #uuid "98ec4914-a43d-55aa-9b2f-cbf99a119ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505403} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b827e3-4c97-500b-86e9-6831c7386dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (1,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f1b2c7f-28a2-5287-9aca-dc1c26ca1232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505262} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a4309c-fb5f-55a2-bf19-9aaf5274891a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "630a0699-f234-53c6-a49d-ff388f9bf008" :question/math-academy-id "q-6734" :question/problem "![](images/40/4091e1bee1b036185a54499839b356f8fce38e864d8f390915e855b26130e998.png)\n\nWhat is the domain of the function $f(x)$ given above?" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ above is defined for values between $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=1,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- On the other hand, because there is an open circle at $x=4,$ this point is not included in the domain.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n1\\le x<4.\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write this as\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dea9cc3-e655-5f03-80a7-beb0e3f252de" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Finite Domain of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210be75c-0a59-5e12-98cb-2b93c04ffa4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [1,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505160} {:answer/id #uuid "26105266-fdf8-5e7e-84a5-e7bd07a284af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [2,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505170} {:answer/id #uuid "3002da76-1787-5945-87ca-71f107662ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505188} {:answer/id #uuid "311f56c5-9585-5a8f-8e7f-73c086425ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [3,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505190} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ae3342-6361-57b1-8ba7-6a0afffc75ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "210be75c-0a59-5e12-98cb-2b93c04ffa4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [1,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505160} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec11523f-1c50-54f4-a06f-16594bae4b40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94dd0256-e111-5129-838c-0c9af377631d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6785" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f123b77781ae6c6665482418480605e8c61455d637dd92b8c929fa8ace7da3e.png)\n\nWhat is the domain of the function given above?" :question/worked-solution "The function above is defined for values between $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=1,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=4,$ this point is also included in the domain.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can write this as\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4].\n$$"}]}
